course: dictionary
lesson: 919
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: One word can have several meanings. Blaze is a good example: it can be a big fire, a bright show of colour, a white mark on an animal, or a way of saying someone was first. Context tells you which meaning you need.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The fire blazed in the hearth» is cosy, so blazed is a verb meaning burned brightly. In «a blaze of colour», blaze is a noun: a very bright display, like the flowers that seemed to have «less of richness and blaze of colour» than they remembered.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning. "A bright display of colour" fits «blaze of colour» and the sentence still makes sense. "Be the first to do something new" would not fit there at all, so you know it is a different meaning. Always test your choice.
fact: One word can have more than one meaning, so always check the context.
fact: «Blaze» can mean a large, strong fire that is often dangerous.
fact: «Blaze» can also mean a very bright light or colour, as in «a blaze of colour».
fact: «Blaze a trail» means to be the first to do something new.
fact: A white stripe down a horse's face is also called a blaze, and so is a mark cut on a tree to show a path.
exercise: The firefighters ran to tackle the blaze. What does blaze mean here? | A bright colour in a painting | A mark cut on a tree | *A large, dangerous fire | A white stripe down a horse's face
exercise: «The sun set in a blaze of orange and red.» What does blaze mean? | To be the first to try something | A mark cut on a tree to show a path | A cold, damp wind | *A very bright display of colour
exercise: Ruby says she will blaze a trail on the new climbing route. What does blaze a trail mean? | To paint a stripe on a tree | To shout loudly across the valley | To set fire to the path | *To be the first to do something new
test: «The fire blazed in the hearth all evening.» What does blazed mean here? | Went out quickly | Shone with a white stripe | Was first to finish the race | *Burned brightly and strongly
test: A horse had a white blaze running down its face. What does blaze mean here? | *A white stripe down the face of an animal | A dangerous fire | To be the first to do something new | A bright colour
exam: Which sentence uses blaze to mean a very bright display? | The dog ran to the gate and barked | *The garden was a blaze of colour in June | The clock struck nine after dinner | Ben will blaze the trail up the hill tomorrow
